1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.chinaknowledge.de/History/Myth/personszhuanxu.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Zhuan Xu 顓頊, also called Gao Yang 高陽, is one of the mythical Five Emperors 五帝. Gao Yang "High Brilliance" is the honorific title of Zhuan Xu. In the southern poetry collection Chuci 楚辞 he is seen as ancestor of the rulers of Chu 楚
2 CONC . A story in the Shanhaijing 山海經 says that he was a descendant of the Yellow Emperor 黃帝 and his consort Lei Zu 雷祖 from the lands Ruoshui 若水, Chaoyun 朝雲 and Siyi 司彘 in the far west. Lei Zu gave birth to Chang Yi 昌意, Chang Yi father
2 CONC ed Han Liu 韓流, a half-human creature parts of whose body were that of animals. Han Liu married Zhuozi 淖子, also called Anü 阿女, who was the mother of Zhuan Xu. The book Diwang shiji 帝王世紀 says that Zhuan Xu was a son of Chang Yi and 
2 CONC Jingpu 景僕 (also called Nüshu 女樞), a daughter of Lord Shushan 蜀山氏. Zhuan Xu was born during the late years of the reign of Lord Jintian 金天氏 (i.e. Shao Hao 少皞). With the age of ten he already helped Emperor Shao Hao in government af
2 CONC fairs, was capped with the age of 12 and mounted the throne with 20, ruling from Diqiu 帝丘 (modern Puyang 濮陽, Henan). Zhuan Xu is in the book Guoyu 國語 credited with the correct positioning of stars and sun towards the north. Riding
2 CONC  on a dragon, he inspected the whole empire and was received by all subjects, including nature, with the greatest respect, as the Da Dai Liji 大戴禮記 says. He is astronomically connected with the direction north and therefore also ca
2 CONC lled the "Black Emperor" (Xuan Di 玄帝 or Hei Di 黑帝). His sons Zhong 重 and Li 黎 (see Zhong Li 重黎) supported him in the fixing of Heaven and Earth, as the Shanhaijing says. According to the Guoyu 國語, Zhong was appointed "corrector of
2 CONC  the south" (nanzheng 南正) and was responsible for the sacrifices to Heaven and the spirits, while Li was appointed "corrector of the hearth fires" (huozheng 火正) or "corrector of the north" (beizheng 北正) and was resonsible for the 
2 CONC administration of the human households. Zhuan Xu is said to have ruled for 78 years. According to another story, he had eight sons, namely Cang Shu 蒼舒, Kui Ai 隤敳, Daoyan 禱戭, Da Lin 大臨, Long Jiang 龍降, Ting Jian 庭堅 (or Gao Yao 皋陶), 
2 CONC Zhong Rong 仲容, and Shu Da 叔達. All of them later became ministers of Emperor Shun 舜. Zhuan Xu was succeeded by Emperor Di Ku 帝嚳. A hill in Gaoyang 高陽, Hebei, is said to be his tomb mound.
2 CONT  The Guoyu tells a story that when the spiritual power of Emperor Shao Hao declined, the Nine Li 九黎 (sons of Zhuan Xu?) brought the whole world into turmoil, so that the security of daily life of the southern Miao 苗 people (at tha
2 CONC t time a common reference for southerners) was endangered and the altars were deserted. Zhuan Xu thereupon restored order and punished Zhong and Li. A similar story is told in the Shangshu 尚書, where the distance between humans the
2 CONC  and gods gave Chi You 蚩尤 the opportunity to rebel against the Yellow Emperor. Yet Zhong and Li are here again seen as supporters of order and virtue that is claimed by the Yellow Emperor and Zhuan Xu (called huangdi 皇帝 or shangdi
2 CONC  上帝 in these stories, without referring to concrete names). In the Shanhaijing Zhong and Li are called grandsons of Zhuan Xu and sons of Lao Tong 老童. While Zhong was ordered to bring tributes to Heaven, Li was ordered to appease t
2 CONC he earth. Much later, the collection Soushenji 搜神記 speaks of three sons of Zhuan Xu that died and became demons of pestilence (yigui 疫鬼), the first was Nüegui 瘧鬼, the second Wanglianggui 魍魎鬼, and the third professed in threatening
2 CONC  small children and was therefore called Xiaoergui 小兒鬼. The Shenyijing 神異經 reports the story of a fierce half-human in the western regions with the name of Daowu 禱杌, which might likewise be an evil son of Zhuan Xu. In the book Dud
2 CONC uan 獨斷, Zhuan Xu is called the god of pestilence (yishen 疫神). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT  Li Jianping 李劍平 (ed. 1998). Zhongguo shenhua renwu cidian 中國神話人物辭典, p. 508. Xi'an: Shaanxi renmin chubanshe.
2 CONT  Xiong Tieji 熊鐵基, Yang Youli 楊有禮 (ed. 1994). Zhongguo diwang zaixiang cidian 中國帝王宰相辭典, p. 7. Wuhan: Hubei jiaoyu chubanshe.
2 CONT  Yang Liangcai 楊亮才 (ed. 1989). Zhongguo minjian wenyi cidian 中國民間文藝辭典,p. 531. Lanzhou: Gansu renmin chubanshe.
2 CONT  Yuan Ke 袁珂 (ed. 1985). Zhongguo shenhua chuanshuo cidian 中國神話傳說詞典, pp. 384-385, 427. Shanghai: Shanghai cishu chubanshe. Zhang Huizhi 張撝之, Shen Qihui 沈起煒, Liu Dezhong 劉德重 (ed. 1999). Zhongguo lidai renming da cidian 中國歷代人名大辭典, vo
2 CONC l. 2, p. 2504. Shanghai: Shanghai guji chubanshe.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.chinaknowledge.org/History/Myth/wudi-rulers.html
2 CONT
2 CONT The Five Mythical Emperors (Wudi) 五帝 
2 CONT
2 CONT name residence
2 CONT
2 CONT (reign time) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Huang Di (Huangdi), the Yellow Emperor 黃帝, surname Xuanyuan 軒轅氏 Youxiong 有熊 (100 years) 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Zhi 帝摯, surname Shaohao 少昊氏, called Qing 清)
2 CONT
2 CONT This person is not by all historians accepted as one of the mythical emperors. Xifang 西方 
2 CONT
2 CONT Zhuan Xu (Zhuanxu) 顓頊 , surname Gaoyang 高陽氏 Pu 濮 (78 years) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Di Ku (Diku) 帝嚳, surname Gaoxin 高辛氏 Bo 亳 (63 years) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Yao 帝堯, surname Tang 唐 or Taotang 陶唐氏, called Fangxun 放勛 Ji 冀 (100 years) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shun 帝舜, surname Yu 虞 or Youyu 有虞氏, called Chonghua 重華 Ji (50 years) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Old Chinese sources often speak of the "Three Augusts" and "Five Emperors" (Sanhuang Wudi 三皇五帝), but scholars disagree who these persons are: 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Three Augusts (Sanhuang 三皇) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fu Xi (Fuxi) 伏羲 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shen Nong (Shennong) 神農 
2 CONT
2 CONT Huang Di (Huangdi) 黃帝, Nü Wa (Nüwa) 女媧, Sui Ren (Suiren) 燧人, Zhu Rong (Zhurong) 祝融 or Gong Gong (Gonggong) 共工 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Five Emperors (Wudi 五帝), different versions 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shao Hao (Shaohao) 少昊 or Huang Di, Gao Yang (Gaoyang) 高陽=Zhuan Xu (Zhuanxu) 顓頊, Gao Xin (Gaoxin) 高辛=Di Ku (Diku) 帝嚳, Yao 堯 and Shun 舜 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tai Hao (Taihao) 太昊, Yan Di (Yandi) 炎帝, Huang Di, Shao Hao and Zhuan Xu 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mi Xi (Mixi) 宓戯 (or 宓戲)=Fu Xi, Shen Nong, Huang Di, Yao and Shun 
2 CONT
2 CONT Huang Di, Shao Hao, Di Ku, Yao, Shun 
2 CONT
2 CONT Zhuan Xu (Gaoyang) is said to be a grandson of the Yellow Emperor (his father being Chang Yi [Changyi] 昌意), Di Ku a great-grandson of the Yellow Emperor (his father being Jiao Ji [Jiaoji] 蟜極, his grand-father being Xuan Ao [Xuan'a
2 CONC o] 玄囂), Yao a son of Di Ku, and Shun a descendant of Zhuan Xu (his father being Gu Sou [Gusou] 瞽叟, his grandfather Jiao Niu [Jiaoniu] 蟜牛, his great-grandfather Juwang [Juwang] or Gou Wang [Gouwang] 句望, whose father was Jing Kang [
2 CONC Jingkang] 敬康, his father Qiong Chan [Qiongchan] 窮蟬, his father Zhuan Xu). 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://history.cultural-china.com/en/46History3800.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Zhuan Xu was a son of Chang Yi and grand-son of the Yellow Emperor. He resided in Diqiu or Pu, his surname of Gaoyang (might also be a place name). He is said to have deepened the knowledge of calendar and astronomy, used 
2 CONC ghosts and spirits for assistance in his government - over a realm that covered the surface of modern China, thought the people by refining the universal breath and installed regulated rituals and sacrifices. One of his sons was Q
2 CONC iong Chan (also called Qiong Xi), ancestor of Emperor Shun.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhuanxu
2 CONT
2 CONT Zhuanxu (simplified Chinese: 颛顼; traditional Chinese: 顓頊; pinyin: Zhuānxū), also known as Gaoyang (高陽) is a monarch of ancient China.
2 CONT
2 CONT A grandson of the Yellow Emperor, Zhuanxu led the Shi clan in an eastward migration to present-day Shandong, where intermarriages with the Dongyi clan enlarged and augmented their tribal influences. At age twenty, he became their 
2 CONC sovereign, going on to rule for seventy-eight years until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He made contributions to a unified calendar, astrology, religion reforms to oppose shamanism, upheld the patriarchal (as opposed to the previous matriarchal) system, and forbade close-kin marriage. Zhuanxu is held by many to be on
2 CONC e of the Five Emperors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zhuanxu was succeeded by his cousin's son, Ku. In the Shiji, he criticized one of his sons for being a dullard. Since only two sons were named, it might have been Gun, father of Yu the Great or Qiongchan, the ancestor of Shun. Yao
2 CONC  had also criticized Gun for being incompetent and ruinous. The Shiji labelled Qiongchan an insignificant commoner though it does not mention how he fell from grace. He also had eight unnamed sons of good repute that later worked 
2 CONC for Shun.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhuanxu
